<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Desmond Tutu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Archbishop Desmond Tutu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Desmond Tutu was a South African Anglican cleric and anti-apartheid campaigner, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> from 1986 to 1996. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1984 for his non-violent opposition to apartheid. After 1994 he chaired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, which heard testimony from victims and perpetrators of apartheid-era abuses, and he popularised the phrase &quot;Rainbow Nation&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu.jpg" alt="Desmond Tutu" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benny Gool · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishop-Tutu-medium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Desmond Mpilo Tutu was born on 7 October 1931 in Klerksdorp, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>, to a Motswana mother and a father from the amaFengu branch of the Xhosa. His father was principal of a Methodist primary school. His parents could not afford medical school, and he trained as a teacher at Pretoria Bantu Normal College from 1951. After the Bantu Education Act of 1953 he and his wife left teaching. With Huddleston's support he chose to become an Anglican priest and entered St Peter's Theological College in Rosettenville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tutu was ordained in 1961 and studied theology at King's College London from 1962. After returning in 1966 he taught at the Federal Theological Seminary in Alice, where he was the first black member of staff, and then directed the Theological Education Fund for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1972. In 1975 he became the first black dean of St Mary's Cathedral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, living in Soweto rather than the dean's residence in a white suburb, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a>. From 1978 to 1985 he was general secretary of the South African Council of Churches.</p><p>He was the first black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, enthroned in February 1985, and called on the international community in his inaugural sermon to introduce economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> unless apartheid was dismantled within 18 to 24 months.</p><p>In September 1986 he was enthroned as Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, again the first black man in the post. After the 1994 election <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> chose him to chair the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-south-africa">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a>, and he retired as archbishop in 1996. Mandela awarded him the Order for Meritorious Service at a farewell ceremony in June 1996. He stressed non-violent protest and foreign economic pressure as ways to bring about universal suffrage, while warning the government that anger at apartheid would lead to racial violence. He was diagnosed with prostate cancer in January 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tutu died on 26 December 2021. Theologically he sought to fuse ideas from black theology with African theology. He was popular among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s black majority and internationally praised for his activism, but many white South Africans considered him too radical, some anti-apartheid activists thought him too moderate, and Jewish groups accused him of making antisemitic statements. After apartheid he campaigned for gay rights and spoke on a wide range of subjects, and he retired from public life in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Desmond Tutu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by <b>Archbishop Desmond Tutu</b> from 1996, became a model studied by many societies emerging from conflict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The churches gave the movement shelter and a voice: <b>Desmond Tutu</b>, general secretary of the South African Council of Churches and from 1986 Archbishop of Cape Town, received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984, and the Afrikaner minister Beyers Naudé left the ministry of his church to found…</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Munsieville, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.08,27.75&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Munsieville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5283 3122 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Desmond Tutu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5733 3392h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5733 3392h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.08,27.75&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
